      "updated": 1780733191666,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Video codec: A video codec is a method that compresses and decompresses\nvideo so it takes less space and bandwidth. It removes repeated or less\nimportant visual information while trying to keep the video looking good.\nDifferent codecs trade off encoding speed, device support, how small the file\nis, and visual quality. Common codecs: H.264, H.265/HEVC, VP9, AV1, MPEG-2.\n\n2) Video container: A video container is the file format that holds the\ncompressed video, audio, subtitles, and metadata in one file. Think of it as\nthe outer box; the codec is how the contents inside are packed. One\ncontainer can hold many different codecs, so the file extension alone does\nnot tell you which codec is used. Common containers: MP4, MKV, WebM, MOV,\nAVI.\n\n3) Bitrate: Bitrate is how much data per second is used to represent the\nvideo, usually measured in kbps or Mbps. Higher bitrate usually means better\npotential quality, but also larger files and more bandwidth use. The right\nbitrate depends on resolution, frame rate, how complex the video content is,\nand how efficient the codec is. Bitrate can be constant (CBR) or variable\n(VBR).\n\n4) Manifest files: Modern streaming does not send one big video file. Instead,\nthe video is split into short segments and each version (different resolution\nand bitrate) is listed in text-based manifest files. The primary (master)\nmanifest lists all available versions of the video and points to media (variant)\nmanifests. Each media manifest lists the URLs of the short segments for\none specific quality level. The player reads the primary manifest, chooses a\nquality level, downloads segments in order, and can switch between qualities\nas network conditions change. This system is called adaptive bitrate\nstreaming (ABR) and is used in HLS and MPEG-DASH.\n\n5) How they fit together: Codec: how the video is compressed. Container:\nwhat file format holds video, audio, and metadata. Bitrate: how much data\nper second is used, effecting quality and bandwidth. Manifest file:\ninstructions/index telling the player what versions exist and which segments\nto download and play.",
      "fontSize": 20.273538308073675,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Video codec: A video codec is a method that compresses and decompresses video so it takes less space and bandwidth. It removes repeated or less important visual information while trying to keep the video looking good. Different codecs trade off encoding speed, device support, how small the file is, and visual quality. Common codecs: H.264, H.265/HEVC, VP9, AV1, MPEG-2.\n\n2) Video container: A video container is the file format that holds the compressed video, audio, subtitles, and metadata in one file. Think of it as the outer box; the codec is how the contents inside are packed. One container can hold many different codecs, so the file extension alone does not tell you which codec is used. Common containers: MP4, MKV, WebM, MOV, AVI.\n\n3) Bitrate: Bitrate is how much data per second is used to represent the video, usually measured in kbps or Mbps. Higher bitrate usually means better potential quality, but also larger files and more bandwidth use. The right bitrate depends on resolution, frame rate, how complex the video content is, and how efficient the codec is. Bitrate can be constant (CBR) or variable (VBR).\n\n4) Manifest files: Modern streaming does not send one big video file. Instead, the video is split into short segments and each version (different resolution and bitrate) is listed in text-based manifest files. The primary (master) manifest lists all available versions of the video and points to media (variant) manifests. Each media manifest lists the URLs of the short segments for one specific quality level. The player reads the primary manifest, chooses a quality level, downloads segments in order, and can switch between qualities as network conditions change. This system is called adaptive bitrate streaming (ABR) and is used in HLS and MPEG-DASH.\n\n5) How they fit together: Codec: how the video is compressed. Container: what file format holds video, audio, and metadata. Bitrate: how much data per second is used, effecting quality and bandwidth. Manifest file: instructions/index telling the player what versions exist and which segments to download and play.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
