      "updated": 1780853893057,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Microservices Architecture Flow (Based on Diagram)\n- The system is broken down into stateless microservices (Read, User,\nUpload, Stream) so each can scale independently based on traffic.\n- Upload Flow: [Client] -> [API Gateway] -> [Upload SVC] -> [Generates\nPre-signed URL]. The client then uploads the raw file directly to [S3].\n- Read Flow: [Client] -> [API Gateway] -> [Read SVC] -> [Cache] ->\n[Database].\n- Stream Flow: [Client] -> [CDN] -> [S3].\n\n2) Database Scaling & Caching Strategy\n- Read Replicas: Since the system is read-heavy, we use a Leader-Follower\nDB architecture. All writes (new songs, new users) go to the Leader. All\nreads (searching, fetching metadata) hit the Follower databases to prevent\nbottlenecks.\n- Sharding: As the catalog grows, we partition the database geographically\n(e.g., US shard, EU shard) or by Artist ID ranges to keep related data\ngrouped together and queries fast.\n- Cache Management: We place a caching layer (like Redis) in front of the\ndatabase for lightning-fast search and metadata retrieval. We use an LRU\n(Least Recently Used) eviction policy so when the cache fills up, the oldest,\nleast-played data is removed to make room for trending songs.\n\n3) Upload & Streaming Optimizations (CDN & Chunking)\n- Global CDNs: We deploy Content Delivery Networks globally (North\nAmerica, Europe, etc.). Instead of fetching audio from the main S3 bucket\nevery time, users stream it from an edge server physically closest to them,\nkilling latency.\n- Chunked & Resumable Uploads: Large audio files are broken into smaller\nchunks during upload. We use file fingerprinting to track which chunks have\nsuccessfully uploaded. If the user's internet drops, it acts as a \"save\npoint,\" allowing a multipart upload to resume exactly where it left off\ninstead of restarting from 0%.\n\n4) Asynchronous Transcoding via Event-Driven Architecture (EDA)\n- Processing audio is heavy. We do not want users waiting on a loading\nscreen while their song processes.\n- The Flow: When a raw file lands in S3, it publishes an event. A message\nbroker routes this event to a fleet of backend worker nodes (e.g., utilizing\nKafka consumer groups). These workers operate in the background,\nasynchronously transcoding the raw file into adaptive bitrates (64kbps,\n128kbps, 320kbps) without blocking the main API.\n\n5) Client-Side Resiliency & Playback\n- Predictive Buffering (Pre-fetching): The mobile client anticipates the\nuser's needs by silently downloading the next few audio chunks (or the next\nsong in a playlist) in the background. If the user drives through a tunnel\nand loses signal, the song continues playing smoothly from the local buffer.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Microservices Architecture Flow (Based on Diagram)\n- The system is broken down into stateless microservices (Read, User, Upload, Stream) so each can scale independently based on traffic.\n- Upload Flow: [Client] -> [API Gateway] -> [Upload SVC] -> [Generates Pre-signed URL]. The client then uploads the raw file directly to [S3].\n- Read Flow: [Client] -> [API Gateway] -> [Read SVC] -> [Cache] -> [Database].\n- Stream Flow: [Client] -> [CDN] -> [S3].\n\n2) Database Scaling & Caching Strategy\n- Read Replicas: Since the system is read-heavy, we use a Leader-Follower DB architecture. All writes (new songs, new users) go to the Leader. All reads (searching, fetching metadata) hit the Follower databases to prevent bottlenecks.\n- Sharding: As the catalog grows, we partition the database geographically (e.g., US shard, EU shard) or by Artist ID ranges to keep related data grouped together and queries fast.\n- Cache Management: We place a caching layer (like Redis) in front of the database for lightning-fast search and metadata retrieval. We use an LRU (Least Recently Used) eviction policy so when the cache fills up, the oldest, least-played data is removed to make room for trending songs.\n\n3) Upload & Streaming Optimizations (CDN & Chunking)\n- Global CDNs: We deploy Content Delivery Networks globally (North America, Europe, etc.). Instead of fetching audio from the main S3 bucket every time, users stream it from an edge server physically closest to them, killing latency.\n- Chunked & Resumable Uploads: Large audio files are broken into smaller chunks during upload. We use file fingerprinting to track which chunks have successfully uploaded. If the user's internet drops, it acts as a \"save point,\" allowing a multipart upload to resume exactly where it left off instead of restarting from 0%.\n\n4) Asynchronous Transcoding via Event-Driven Architecture (EDA)\n- Processing audio is heavy. We do not want users waiting on a loading screen while their song processes.\n- The Flow: When a raw file lands in S3, it publishes an event. A message broker routes this event to a fleet of backend worker nodes (e.g., utilizing Kafka consumer groups). These workers operate in the background, asynchronously transcoding the raw file into adaptive bitrates (64kbps, 128kbps, 320kbps) without blocking the main API.\n\n5) Client-Side Resiliency & Playback\n- Predictive Buffering (Pre-fetching): The mobile client anticipates the user's needs by silently downloading the next few audio chunks (or the next song in a playlist) in the background. If the user drives through a tunnel and loses signal, the song continues playing smoothly from the local buffer.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
