    {
      "id": "ZkBBWKb1vT2HifWUbXKkq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1575.154490780718,
      "y": 80.89308784110017,
      "width": 732.9198328673299,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bI9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1874489904,
      "version": 519,
      "versionNonce": 74762448,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1780850866169,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Storing 3 PB of data in RAM (like Redis) is financially impossible.\n- The Solution: Instead of calculating cache based on total traffic volume,\ncalculate it based on the hottest catalog items (e.g., caching the top\n20% most-played songs results in a highly manageable 60 TB).\n- The 3-Tier Cache Architecture:\n    > L1 (Client-Side): Zero-cost local storage directly on user devices\n(phones, laptops).\n    > L2 (Edge CDN): The massive 3 PB load is distributed globally using\ncheap disk storage (SSDs/NVMe). Across 200 global locations, this\naverages out to just ~15 TB per edge node.\n    > L3 (Internal RAM): Expensive Redis memory is strictly reserved for\nlightweight, mutable metadata (user profiles, URLs, playlists), keeping the\nRAM footprint to a few hundred gigabytes.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Storing 3 PB of data in RAM (like Redis) is financially impossible.\n- The Solution: Instead of calculating cache based on total traffic volume, calculate it based on the hottest catalog items (e.g., caching the top 20% most-played songs results in a highly manageable 60 TB).\n- The 3-Tier Cache Architecture:\n    > L1 (Client-Side): Zero-cost local storage directly on user devices (phones, laptops).\n    > L2 (Edge CDN): The massive 3 PB load is distributed globally using cheap disk storage (SSDs/NVMe). Across 200 global locations, this averages out to just ~15 TB per edge node.\n    > L3 (Internal RAM): Expensive Redis memory is strictly reserved for lightweight, mutable metadata (user profiles, URLs, playlists), keeping the RAM footprint to a few hundred gigabytes.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
